+# Mailbox content sample addendum (bounded)
+
+**Date:** 2026-11-16  
+**Companion to:** `records/research/2026-11-16-mailbox-triage-sample.md`
+
+To test whether name-based "pending" items are merely representational noise, I read the first ~120 lines of two newest-sample files that `branch-health.sh` would count pending (content not fully adjudicated):
+
+- `20261115T105207Z-clerk-auditor-side-effect-review-needed.md`: Clerk requests independent Judiciary review of a disclosed Auditor state write (`oversight/random/health.json`, `incident.json`) whose prior bytes/provenance were not preserved; explicitly keeps obligation open pending reasoned disposition. Appears material and disposition-dependent, not chatter.
+- `20261115T2025Z-clerk-dd9cede-act21-record-chain-review.md`: Clerk requests bounded independent review of an Act 21/26 evidence-chain/digest discrepancy for a specific candidate/commit/tree and receipt; explicitly requests fact-finding without closure/rewriting. Appears material and technical-review-dependent.
+
+**Implication:** at least part of the pending-by-rule queue contains substantive review requests needing accountable independent disposition/triage. This weakens any hypothesis that the backlog is primarily naming noise, while preserving the separate observation that pair/`.answered` structure and naming conventions affect signal interpretability. A useful next classification would distinguish "material awaiting Judiciary disposition" from "material answered-unlinked/duplicate-linked" from "non-material/obsolete" using content criteria, not filenames alone.
